KG42.register("nodes-fadb4784c886e13f348e77bb",{"id":"KG40-d5743b8980847a21","code":"OMC031.3","label":"Responsibility for organizing system-operation service procurement","definition":"The organizer solicits service supply around power system operation needs, decides procurement allocation, and organizes result delivery; network security, compensation rules, and penalty management are retained according to the source.","layer":"L4","template_code":"JOC","concept_id":"coordination.local_operator","concept_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Responsibility and authority: the organizer decides system service procurement and allocation","Economic object: flexibility services that meet network operation needs","Inputs and actions: operation needs and service submissions enter procurement allocation"]},"exclusions":[["The DSO is the organizer, and the asset aggregator is the service provider.","Check network constraints according to expected demand; when the check does not pass, solicit flexibility submissions.","Form and communicate allocation instructions through problem (11).","Security responsibility is expressed by upper and lower bounds on node voltage and line current.","Design ECPM compensation rules, and select or adjust penalty parameters such as beta and gamma."]],"members":[{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The DSO performs local flexibility allocation by solving problem (11) given the aggregators’ bids. Scope: Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system. Condition: The network-constraint check fails and aggregators have submitted flexibility bids. The allocation objective is to minimize the highest aggregator flexibility cost while satisfying the distribution system’s operational constraints. Scope: Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system. | Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E19"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"e03dd623fdbb063aed9525656a6b9287e2943202a2b70919f533e9166984a2dc","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001","node_id":"coordination.local_operator","function":"coordination.local_operator","unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The DSO may acquire flexibility services from asset aggregators to maintain network voltages and currents within safe limits. Scope: Proposed distribution-system flexibility market for asset aggregators. The DSO is responsible for operating the distribution system so that voltage and current magnitudes remain within safe limits. Scope: DSO responsibility for operating the distribution system served by the flexibility market.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"DSO responsibility for operating the distribution system served by the flexibility market. | Proposed distribution-system flexibility market for asset aggregators.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E28"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","source_rule_sha256":"f9c38dc6db59cf489bc5dfc1d045ca3c67527afcee1910531f185ffa384fa42e","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002","node_id":"coordination.local_operator","function":"coordination.local_operator","unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction. Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. | DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. | ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty. | Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. | Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. | Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"e2e1bbf6c09250fdda3484fd4e5edbd04edd283e47fe691fc6021e384f76eaa3","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003","node_id":"coordination.local_operator","function":"coordination.local_operator","unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false}],"paper_ids":["40-fair_dso_flexibility"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective of the DSO is to satisfy the operational constraints of the distribution system, while minimizing the highest flexibility cost incurred among aggregators by deciding the optimal flexibility activation. In other words, the DSO pursues a fair allocation of flexibility costs among aggregators so that all aggregators are motivated to contribute, while no particular aggregator has to be compromised to unilaterally facilitate the DSO in achieving its objectives.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"The objective of the DSO is to satisfy the operational constraints of the distribution system, while minimizing the highest flexibility cost incurred among aggregators by deciding the optimal flexibility activation. In other words, the DSO pursues a fair allocation of flexibility costs among aggregators so that all aggregators are motivated to contribute, while no particular aggregator has to be compromised to unilaterally facilitate the DSO in achieving its objectives."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p01-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"Abstract—The proliferation of distributed energy assets neces- sitates the provision of ﬂexibility t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Abstract—The proliferation of distributed energy assets necessitates the provision of flexibility to efficiently operate modern distribution systems. In this article, we propose a flexibility market through which the DSO may acquire flexibility services from asset aggregators in order to maintain network voltages and currents within safe limits. A max-min fair formulation is proposed for the allocation of flexibility. Since the DSO is not aware of each aggregator’s local flexibility costs, we show that strategic misreporting can lead to severe loss of efficiency. Using mechanism design theory, we provide a mechanism that makes it a payoff-maximizing strategy for each aggregator to make truthful bids to the flexibility market. While typical truthful mechanisms only work when the objective is the maximization of Social Welfare, the proposed mechanism lets the DSO achieve incentive compatibility and optimality for the max-min fairness objective.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Abstract—The proliferation of distributed energy assets necessitates the provision of flexibility to efficiently operate modern distribution systems. In this article, we propose a flexibility market through which the DSO may acquire flexibility services from asset aggregators in order to maintain network voltages and currents within safe limits. A max-min fair formulation is proposed for the allocation of flexibility. Since the DSO is not aware of each aggregator’s local flexibility costs, we show that strategic misreporting can lead to severe loss of efficiency. Using mechanism design theory, we provide a mechanism that makes it a payoff-maximizing strategy for each aggregator to make truthful bids to the flexibility market. While typical truthful mechanisms only work when the objective is the maximization of Social Welfare, the proposed mechanism lets the DSO achieve incentive compatibility and optimality for the max-min fairness objective."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E28","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"SO FLEXIBILITY MARKETS 2251 n- the day-ahead market and it is communicated by the day-ahead ant mark","equation_id":"(1)-(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The DSO is responsible for the operation of the distribution system, so that voltage and current magnitudes remain within safe limits. For this purpose, the DSO draws on the flexibility offered by the aggregators in \\(\\Omega_b\\), that is, the ability of an aggregator to modify its active power demand \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) at timeslot \\(t\\) by a certain factor \\(K_{i,t}\\). A modification \\(K_{i,t}P^D_{i,t}\\), results in an active power injection \\(P^S_{i,t}\\), determined by the active power balance equation \\(\\sum_{ki\\in\\Omega_l}P_{ki,t}-\\sum_{ij\\in\\Omega_l}(P_{ij,t}+R_{ij}I^{sqr}_{ij,t})+P^S_{i,t}=K_{i,t}P^D_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (1)\\). The corresponding reactive power flows are represented as \\(\\sum_{ki\\in\\Omega_l}Q_{ki,t}-\\sum_{ij\\in\\Omega_l}(Q_{ij,t}+X_{ij}I^{sqr}_{ij,t})+Q^S_{i,t}=K_{i,t}Q^D_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (2)\\). Notice that aggregators are assumed to maintain a constant power factor and also, it is assumed \\(P^S_{i,t}=Q^S_{i,t}=0\\) for all buses but the substation. On the other hand, for the substation bus, notice that \\(P^S_{0,t}\\) can also be less than 0, representing back-feeding. It should be noted that these assumptions are without loss of generality with respect to the methods that will be presented.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The DSO is responsible for the operation of the distribution system, so that voltage and current magnitudes remain within safe limits. For this purpose, the DSO draws on the flexibility offered by the aggregators in \\(\\Omega_b\\), that is, the ability of an aggregator to modify its active power demand \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) at timeslot \\(t\\) by a certain factor \\(K_{i,t}\\). A modification \\(K_{i,t}P^D_{i,t}\\), results in an active power injection \\(P^S_{i,t}\\), determined by the active power balance equation \\(\\sum_{ki\\in\\Omega_l}P_{ki,t}-\\sum_{ij\\in\\Omega_l}(P_{ij,t}+R_{ij}I^{sqr}_{ij,t})+P^S_{i,t}=K_{i,t}P^D_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (1)\\). The corresponding reactive power flows are represented as \\(\\sum_{ki\\in\\Omega_l}Q_{ki,t}-\\sum_{ij\\in\\Omega_l}(Q_{ij,t}+X_{ij}I^{sqr}_{ij,t})+Q^S_{i,t}=K_{i,t}Q^D_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (2)\\). Notice that aggregators are assumed to maintain a constant power factor and also, it is assumed \\(P^S_{i,t}=Q^S_{i,t}=0\\) for all buses but the substation. On the other hand, for the substation bus, notice that \\(P^S_{0,t}\\) can also be less than 0, representing back-feeding. It should be noted that these assumptions are without loss of generality with respect to the methods that will be presented."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"presented. Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as hat squared, non-negative, conti","equation_id":"(3)-(7)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as squared, non-negative, continuous variables in the steady-state operation of AC electrical distribution systems, it is convenient to perform the change in variables \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\equiv V_{i,t}^2\\) and \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\equiv I_{ij,t}^2\\) as in [35]. The voltage magnitude drop between nodes \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) is represented by \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}-2(R_{ij}P_{ij,t}+X_{ij}Q_{ij,t})-(R_{ij}^2+X_{ij}^2)I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=V^{sqr}_{j,t},\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (3)\\), while current magnitudes are calculated using \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (4)\\). Upper and lower bounds on nodal voltage magnitudes and current magnitudes are enforced by \\(\\underline V^2\\le V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\le\\overline V^2,\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (5)\\) and \\(0\\le I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\le\\overline I_{ij}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (6)\\). An aggregator’s flexibility is bounded as in \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\overline K_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (7)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as squared, non-negative, continuous variables in the steady-state operation of AC electrical distribution systems, it is convenient to perform the change in variables \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\equiv V_{i,t}^2\\) and \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\equiv I_{ij,t}^2\\) as in [35]. The voltage magnitude drop between nodes \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) is represented by \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}-2(R_{ij}P_{ij,t}+X_{ij}Q_{ij,t})-(R_{ij}^2+X_{ij}^2)I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=V^{sqr}_{j,t},\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (3)\\), while current magnitudes are calculated using \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (4)\\). Upper and lower bounds on nodal voltage magnitudes and current magnitudes are enforced by \\(\\underline V^2\\le V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\le\\overline V^2,\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (5)\\) and \\(0\\le I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\le\\overline I_{ij}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (6)\\). An aggregator’s flexibility is bounded as in \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\overline K_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (7)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E32","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p01-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"NOMENCLATURE","anchor_text":"NOMENCLATURE Sets A er b Set of nodes/aggregators. Fl l Set of lines. tow T Set of time periods. ati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Sets: \\(\\Omega_b\\) Set of nodes/aggregators. \\(\\Omega_l\\) Set of lines. \\(\\Omega_T\\) Set of time periods. Parameters: \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) Parameter of flexibility cost at node i, period t [$/kWh^2]. \\(\\overline K_{i,t}\\) Maximum flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\) Minimum flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(\\overline I_{ij}\\) Maximum current magnitude for line ij [A]. \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) Active power demand at node i, period t [kW]. \\(Q^D_{i,t}\\) Reactive power demand at node i, period t [kvar]. \\(R_{ij}\\) Resistance of line ij [m\\Omega]. \\(\\overline V\\) Maximum voltage magnitude [kV]. \\(\\underline V\\) Minimum voltage magnitude [kV]. \\(X_{ij}\\) Reactance of line ij [m\\Omega].","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Sets: \\(\\Omega_b\\) Set of nodes/aggregators. \\(\\Omega_l\\) Set of lines. \\(\\Omega_T\\) Set of time periods. Parameters: \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) Parameter of flexibility cost at node i, period t [$/kWh^2]. \\(\\overline K_{i,t}\\) Maximum flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\) Minimum flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(\\overline I_{ij}\\) Maximum current magnitude for line ij [A]. \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) Active power demand at node i, period t [kW]. \\(Q^D_{i,t}\\) Reactive power demand at node i, period t [kvar]. \\(R_{ij}\\) Resistance of line ij [m\\Omega]. \\(\\overline V\\) Maximum voltage magnitude [kV]. \\(\\underline V\\) Minimum voltage magnitude [kV]. \\(X_{ij}\\) Reactance of line ij [m\\Omega]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E33","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p01-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"NOMENCLATURE","anchor_text":"es- V Maximum voltage magnitude [kV]. rn V Minimum voltage magnitude [kV]. ar- Xij Reactance of line","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Variables: \\(K_{i,t}\\) Flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\) Squared current magnitude at line ij, period t [A^2]. \\(P_{ij,t}\\) Active power flow at line ij, period t [kW]. \\(P^S_{i,t}\\) Active power injection at substation i, period t [kW]. \\(Q_{ij,t}\\) Reactive power flow at line ij, period t [kvar]. \\(Q^S_{i,t}\\) Reactive power injection at substation i, period t [kvar]. \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\) Squared voltage magnitude at node i, period t [kV^2].","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Variables: \\(K_{i,t}\\) Flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\) Squared current magnitude at line ij, period t [A^2]. \\(P_{ij,t}\\) Active power flow at line ij, period t [kW]. \\(P^S_{i,t}\\) Active power injection at substation i, period t [kW]. \\(Q_{ij,t}\\) Reactive power flow at line ij, period t [kvar]. \\(Q^S_{i,t}\\) Reactive power injection at substation i, period t [kvar]. \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\) Squared voltage magnitude at node i, period t [kV^2]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"re The payoff of an aggregator i, deﬁned as πi, is calculated te, as the compensation pi received by","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The task of the DSO in this case is to design the reward function, \\(p_i\\), in such a way that it makes it a dominant strategy for each aggregator to first make truthful declarations and then follow the flexibility instruction, i.e., \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\approx\\mathbf L_i\\) and \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\approx\\mathbf K_i^*\\), respectively. That is, the reward function \\(p(\\cdot)\\) should be incentive compatible in the sense that it aligns the aggregators’ local objectives with the general objective of the DSO. This needs to be done in order to achieve the optimal solution to problem (11), since this solution guarantees the safe operation of the distribution system and fairness for flexibility assets.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The task of the DSO in this case is to design the reward function, \\(p_i\\), in such a way that it makes it a dominant strategy for each aggregator to first make truthful declarations and then follow the flexibility instruction, i.e., \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\approx\\mathbf L_i\\) and \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\approx\\mathbf K_i^*\\), respectively. That is, the reward function \\(p(\\cdot)\\) should be incentive compatible in the sense that it aligns the aggregators’ local objectives with the general objective of the DSO. This needs to be done in order to achieve the optimal solution to problem (11), since this solution guarantees the safe operation of the distribution system and fairness for flexibility assets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["flexibility_market_organizer"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"KG40-L4-SC-L4-f554fd3826a4840e","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L4","ontological_kind":"SC-L4-f554fd3826a4840e"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"The organizer solicits service supply around power system operation needs, decides procurement allocation, and organizes result delivery; network security, compensation rules, and penalty management are retained according to the source.","identity_fields":["Responsibility and authority: the organizer decides system service procurement and allocation","Economic object: flexibility services that meet network operation needs","Inputs and actions: operation needs and service submissions enter procurement allocation"]},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","paper_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"coordination.local_operator","source_occurrences":[{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The DSO performs local flexibility allocation by solving problem (11) given the aggregators’ bids. Scope: Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system. Condition: The network-constraint check fails and aggregators have submitted flexibility bids. The allocation objective is to minimize the highest aggregator flexibility cost while satisfying the distribution system’s operational constraints. Scope: Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Max-min fair flexibility-allocation optimization within the modeled distribution system. | Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E19"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"e03dd623fdbb063aed9525656a6b9287e2943202a2b70919f533e9166984a2dc"},{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The DSO may acquire flexibility services from asset aggregators to maintain network voltages and currents within safe limits. Scope: Proposed distribution-system flexibility market for asset aggregators. The DSO is responsible for operating the distribution system so that voltage and current magnitudes remain within safe limits. Scope: DSO responsibility for operating the distribution system served by the flexibility market.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"DSO responsibility for operating the distribution system served by the flexibility market. | Proposed distribution-system flexibility market for asset aggregators.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E28"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","source_rule_sha256":"f9c38dc6db59cf489bc5dfc1d045ca3c67527afcee1910531f185ffa384fa42e"},{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.local_operator","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction. Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. | DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. | ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty. | Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. | Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. | Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"e2e1bbf6c09250fdda3484fd4e5edbd04edd283e47fe691fc6021e384f76eaa3"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The DSO performs local flexibility allocation by solving problem (11) given the aggregators’ bids. Scope: Proposed flexibility-market allocation among aggregators within the modeled distribution system. Condition: The network-constraint check fails and aggregators have submitted flexibility bids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E19"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective of the DSO is to satisfy the operational constraints of the distribution system, while minimizing the highest flexibility cost incurred among aggregators by deciding the optimal flexibility activation. In other words, the DSO pursues a fair allocation of flexibility costs among aggregators so that all aggregators are motivated to contribute, while no particular aggregator has to be compromised to unilaterally facilitate the DSO in achieving its objectives."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The DSO may acquire flexibility services from asset aggregators to maintain network voltages and currents within safe limits. Scope: Proposed distribution-system flexibility market for asset aggregators.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p01-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"Abstract—The proliferation of distributed energy assets neces- sitates the provision of ﬂexibility t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Abstract—The proliferation of distributed energy assets necessitates the provision of flexibility to efficiently operate modern distribution systems. In this article, we propose a flexibility market through which the DSO may acquire flexibility services from asset aggregators in order to maintain network voltages and currents within safe limits. A max-min fair formulation is proposed for the allocation of flexibility. Since the DSO is not aware of each aggregator’s local flexibility costs, we show that strategic misreporting can lead to severe loss of efficiency. Using mechanism design theory, we provide a mechanism that makes it a payoff-maximizing strategy for each aggregator to make truthful bids to the flexibility market. While typical truthful mechanisms only work when the objective is the maximization of Social Welfare, the proposed mechanism lets the DSO achieve incentive compatibility and optimality for the max-min fairness objective."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The DSO is responsible for operating the distribution system so that voltage and current magnitudes remain within safe limits. Scope: DSO responsibility for operating the distribution system served by the flexibility market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E28","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"SO FLEXIBILITY MARKETS 2251 n- the day-ahead market and it is communicated by the day-ahead ant mark","equation_id":"(1)-(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The DSO is responsible for the operation of the distribution system, so that voltage and current magnitudes remain within safe limits. For this purpose, the DSO draws on the flexibility offered by the aggregators in \\(\\Omega_b\\), that is, the ability of an aggregator to modify its active power demand \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) at timeslot \\(t\\) by a certain factor \\(K_{i,t}\\). A modification \\(K_{i,t}P^D_{i,t}\\), results in an active power injection \\(P^S_{i,t}\\), determined by the active power balance equation \\(\\sum_{ki\\in\\Omega_l}P_{ki,t}-\\sum_{ij\\in\\Omega_l}(P_{ij,t}+R_{ij}I^{sqr}_{ij,t})+P^S_{i,t}=K_{i,t}P^D_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (1)\\). The corresponding reactive power flows are represented as \\(\\sum_{ki\\in\\Omega_l}Q_{ki,t}-\\sum_{ij\\in\\Omega_l}(Q_{ij,t}+X_{ij}I^{sqr}_{ij,t})+Q^S_{i,t}=K_{i,t}Q^D_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (2)\\). Notice that aggregators are assumed to maintain a constant power factor and also, it is assumed \\(P^S_{i,t}=Q^S_{i,t}=0\\) for all buses but the substation. On the other hand, for the substation bus, notice that \\(P^S_{0,t}\\) can also be less than 0, representing back-feeding. It should be noted that these assumptions are without loss of generality with respect to the methods that will be presented."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The DSO's safe-operation responsibility for voltage and current magnitudes is expressed by the source's nodal voltage and line-current bounds: squared voltage magnitude lies between the squared minimum and maximum voltage limits at every node and period, and squared current magnitude lies between zero and the squared maximum line current at every line and period.","expression":"\\underline{V}^{2} \\le V^{sqr}_{i,t} \\le \\overline{V}^{2}, \\quad \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b, t\\in\\Omega_T; \\quad 0 \\le I^{sqr}_{ij,t} \\le \\overline{I}_{ij}^{2}, \\quad \\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l, t\\in\\Omega_T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["V_sqr","I_sqr"],"parameter_refs":["V_min","V_max","I_max"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E33"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"presented. Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as hat squared, non-negative, conti","equation_id":"(3)-(7)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since current and voltage magnitudes appear naturally as squared, non-negative, continuous variables in the steady-state operation of AC electrical distribution systems, it is convenient to perform the change in variables \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\equiv V_{i,t}^2\\) and \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\equiv I_{ij,t}^2\\) as in [35]. The voltage magnitude drop between nodes \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) is represented by \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}-2(R_{ij}P_{ij,t}+X_{ij}Q_{ij,t})-(R_{ij}^2+X_{ij}^2)I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=V^{sqr}_{j,t},\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (3)\\), while current magnitudes are calculated using \\(V^{sqr}_{j,t}I^{sqr}_{ij,t}=P_{ij,t}^2+Q_{ij,t}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (4)\\). Upper and lower bounds on nodal voltage magnitudes and current magnitudes are enforced by \\(\\underline V^2\\le V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\le\\overline V^2,\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (5)\\) and \\(0\\le I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\le\\overline I_{ij}^2,\\quad\\forall ij\\in\\Omega_l,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (6)\\). An aggregator’s flexibility is bounded as in \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\le K_{i,t}\\le\\overline K_{i,t},\\quad\\forall i\\in\\Omega_b,t\\in\\Omega_T\\quad (7)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E32","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p01-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"NOMENCLATURE","anchor_text":"NOMENCLATURE Sets A er b Set of nodes/aggregators. Fl l Set of lines. tow T Set of time periods. ati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Sets: \\(\\Omega_b\\) Set of nodes/aggregators. \\(\\Omega_l\\) Set of lines. \\(\\Omega_T\\) Set of time periods. Parameters: \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) Parameter of flexibility cost at node i, period t [$/kWh^2]. \\(\\overline K_{i,t}\\) Maximum flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\) Minimum flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(\\overline I_{ij}\\) Maximum current magnitude for line ij [A]. \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) Active power demand at node i, period t [kW]. \\(Q^D_{i,t}\\) Reactive power demand at node i, period t [kvar]. \\(R_{ij}\\) Resistance of line ij [m\\Omega]. \\(\\overline V\\) Maximum voltage magnitude [kV]. \\(\\underline V\\) Minimum voltage magnitude [kV]. \\(X_{ij}\\) Reactance of line ij [m\\Omega]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E33","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p01-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"NOMENCLATURE","anchor_text":"es- V Maximum voltage magnitude [kV]. rn V Minimum voltage magnitude [kV]. ar- Xij Reactance of line","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Variables: \\(K_{i,t}\\) Flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(I^{sqr}_{ij,t}\\) Squared current magnitude at line ij, period t [A^2]. \\(P_{ij,t}\\) Active power flow at line ij, period t [kW]. \\(P^S_{i,t}\\) Active power injection at substation i, period t [kW]. \\(Q_{ij,t}\\) Reactive power flow at line ij, period t [kvar]. \\(Q^S_{i,t}\\) Reactive power injection at substation i, period t [kvar]. \\(V^{sqr}_{i,t}\\) Squared voltage magnitude at node i, period t [kV^2]."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"re The payoff of an aggregator i, deﬁned as πi, is calculated te, as the compensation pi received by","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The task of the DSO in this case is to design the reward function, \\(p_i\\), in such a way that it makes it a dominant strategy for each aggregator to first make truthful declarations and then follow the flexibility instruction, i.e., \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\approx\\mathbf L_i\\) and \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\approx\\mathbf K_i^*\\), respectively. That is, the reward function \\(p(\\cdot)\\) should be incentive compatible in the sense that it aligns the aggregators’ local objectives with the general objective of the DSO. This needs to be done in order to achieve the optimal solution to problem (11), since this solution guarantees the safe operation of the distribution system and fairness for flexibility assets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"ECPM payment rule (14): the payment to aggregator i equals the fixed payment R_i plus the observed actual cost, minus beta times the maximum of the observed actual cost and the worst declared cost among other aggregators evaluated at their instructions, minus a quadratic deviation penalty summed over the horizon.","expression":"p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L}) = R_i + \\tilde c_i - \\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\right\\} - \\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_beta","var_gamma","var_R_i","var_tildeK_i"],"parameter_refs":["par_K_i_star","par_tilde_c_i","par_hatL_j","par_c_j_eval","par_Omega_b","par_Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"V_sqr","symbol":"V^{sqr}_{i,t}","meaning":"Squared voltage magnitude at node i in period t.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real","unit":"kV^2","indices":["i ∈ Ω_b","t ∈ Ω_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E33","v141-b-g01-E29"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::x::V_sqr"},{"variable_id":"I_sqr","symbol":"I^{sqr}_{ij,t}","meaning":"Squared current magnitude on line ij in period t.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real","unit":"A^2","indices":["ij ∈ Ω_l","t ∈ Ω_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E33","v141-b-g01-E29"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::x::I_sqr"},{"variable_id":"var_beta","symbol":"\\beta","meaning":"Penalization factor in the compensation-and-penalty payment rule, chosen by the DSO.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; DSO-chosen scalar penalization factor","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_beta"},{"variable_id":"var_gamma","symbol":"\\gamma","meaning":"Per-unit coefficient of the quadratic deviation penalty, adjustable by the DSO.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; DSO-chosen scalar satisfying the stated incentive and participation trade-off","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E25","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_gamma"},{"variable_id":"var_R_i","symbol":"R_i","meaning":"Fixed payment to aggregator i in ECPM, intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; fixed payment amount for aggregator i","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_R_i"},{"variable_id":"var_tildeK_i","symbol":"\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i","meaning":"Actual final flexibility profile implemented by aggregator i and measured by the DSO, possibly differing from the instruction.","kind":"operational","domain":"profile over t in \\Omega_T of measured actual flexibility factors; source does not state explicit numerical bounds for the measured profile","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_tildeK_i"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"V_min","symbol":"\\underline V","meaning":"Minimum voltage magnitude.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kV","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E29"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::theta::V_min"},{"parameter_id":"V_max","symbol":"\\overline V","meaning":"Maximum voltage magnitude.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kV","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E29"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::theta::V_max"},{"parameter_id":"I_max","symbol":"\\overline I_{ij}","meaning":"Maximum current magnitude for line ij.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"A","indices":["ij ∈ Ω_l"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E29"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::theta::I_max"},{"parameter_id":"par_K_i_star","symbol":"\\mathbf K_i^*","meaning":"Optimal flexibility instruction communicated to aggregator i from the clearing problem using the declared tuples.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"clearing instruction"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E06"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_K_i_star"},{"parameter_id":"par_tilde_c_i","symbol":"\\tilde c_i","meaning":"Observed actual flexibility cost of aggregator i after the horizon, used as the actual-cost reimbursement term and in the maximum-cost penalty.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"observed actual cost"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g03-E15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_tilde_c_i"},{"parameter_id":"par_hatL_j","symbol":"\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j","meaning":"Declared parameter tuple of another aggregator j, used in the maximum-cost penalty term.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"declared parameter tuple of aggregator j"},"unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E06"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_hatL_j"},{"parameter_id":"par_c_j_eval","symbol":"c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)","meaning":"Declared flexibility cost of another aggregator j evaluated at its instruction and declared tuple in the maximum-cost penalty.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"declared cost evaluated at instruction"},"unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_c_j_eval"},{"parameter_id":"par_Omega_b","symbol":"\\Omega_b","meaning":"Set of nodes/aggregators over which the maximum-cost penalty and sums are indexed.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of nodes/aggregators"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E31"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_Omega_b"},{"parameter_id":"par_Omega_T","symbol":"\\Omega_T","meaning":"Set of time periods over which the quadratic deviation penalty is summed.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of time periods"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E31"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_Omega_T"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","stage":"L4","subtype":"flexibility_market_organizer","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The DSO is the local flexibility market organizer: it bears responsibility for the safe operation of the distribution network, procures flexibility from aggregators, organizes submissions and allocation instructions, and designs compensation rules and penalty parameters.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: the entity organizing the local flexibility market","Core relationship: responsible for flexibility procurement, allocation coordination, and compensation rule design","Role predicate: an operator that bears network operation responsibility and exercises the above organizational responsibilities"],"variation_fields":["The organizing entity is the DSO, and the service provider is the asset aggregator","Check network constraints based on expected demand; when the check does not pass, solicit flexibility submissions","Form and communicate allocation instructions through problem (11)","Security responsibility is expressed by upper and lower bounds on node voltage and line current","Design ECPM compensation rules, and select or adjust penalty parameters such as beta and gamma"],"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007"],"paper_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"coordination.local_operator","template_code":"JOC"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","retained_variants":["The DSO is the organizer, and the asset aggregator is the service provider.","Check network constraints according to expected demand; when the check does not pass, solicit flexibility submissions.","Form and communicate allocation instructions through problem (11).","Security responsibility is expressed by upper and lower bounds on node voltage and line current.","Design ECPM compensation rules, and select or adjust penalty parameters such as beta and gamma."],"evidence_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator::projection"]}],"functional":{"is_child":true,"subdivision_pending":false,"baseline_parent_id":"COMP-488e4abbbcabfe42","baseline_parent_code":"OMC031","secondary_class":"SC-L4-f554fd3826a4840e","category":{"id":"SC-L4-f554fd3826a4840e","stage":"L4","label_zh":"System operation service procurement organization","definition_zh":"The organizer solicits supply submissions according to power system operation service needs, decides the resources to accept or the procurement allocation, and organizes result delivery; security, compensation, and control authority are separately limited according to the source.","main_identity_axes":["Decision authority: the organizer decides service acceptance and procurement allocation","Economic object: services that meet system operation needs","Inputs, actions, and outputs: service needs and submissions enter procurement selection and allocation arrangements"],"inclusion_criteria":["Explicitly organizes service procurement for power system operation needs","The organizer has the authority to decide on submission acceptance, resource selection, or the extent of procurement"],"exclusion_criteria":["Only organizes clearing of a general bilateral energy market","Only checks network status or forwards service requests","Only implements predetermined compensation payments","Only describes direct resource control, without clarifying service procurement organization"],"supporting_unit_ids":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-a-M03-ancillary-service::coordination.local_operator"],"status":"agent_defined_structurally_validated"},"definition":{"definition_zh":"The organizer solicits service supply around power system operation needs, decides procurement allocation, and organizes result delivery; network security, compensation rules, and penalty management are retained according to the source.","identity_fields":["Responsibility and authority: the organizer decides system service procurement and allocation","Economic object: flexibility services that meet network operation needs","Inputs and actions: operation needs and service submissions enter procurement allocation"],"member_unit_ids":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::coordination.local_operator"]}},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/90"});
